# Worktree Isolation

## Rule

Never edit host checkout files for implementation work. Start each task in an isolated git worktree.

## Setup

```bash
git worktree add -b <branch-name> .worktrees/<task-name> <base-branch>
```

- Always branch from the repo's default branch
- Keep host checkout read-only (exploration and review only)
- All edits, builds, and tests happen inside the worktree directory

## Before Starting

1. Check for parallel PRs touching the same area
2. Create worktree from host root
3. Verify branch ancestry is correct
4. Bootstrap tracked env files from the host checkout into the worktree when they exist on the base branch (do not assume they are absent just because a fresh worktree does not include them)

## Tracked Env Bootstrap

- If the repository tracks env files in `main`/base, copy them into the worktree before builds, tests, or deploy checks
- In this repo, explicitly check `backend/.env` and `frontend/.env`
- Keep host checkout read-only for implementation, but use it as the source of truth for tracked env files that must also exist in the worktree

## Branch Naming (non-blocking)

`<github-alias>/<jira-id>/<short-description>`

## Execution Attestation (ARO-26594)

When working as an agent, produce verifiable proof of worktree-based execution:
- Record the worktree path and host checkout path at session start
- Confirm no files were modified in the host checkout directory
- CI should validate that the PR was authored from an isolated worktree

## Cleanup

Remove worktrees after merging:
```bash
git worktree remove .worktrees/<task-name>
```
